{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790268825,"nonce":"Oq8Sm5w50kOMXDsGKcmevQP/jlQ=","title":"Python: a parameter named range is ignored by a for loop over range(...)","message":"`for_with_body` recognises a counted loop by the literal name `range` without asking whether the name is bound locally, so a parameter or local named `range` is bypassed and the builtin counts instead.\n\n    def f(range):\n        out = []\n        for i in range(3):\n            out.append(i)\n        return out\n    print(f(lambda n: [9, 8]))   # CPython [9, 8]; zypy [0, 1, 2]\n\nThe same holds when a module alias `r = range` is read inside such a function after the alias rewrite. The enumerate arm already checks `!self.is_variable(\"enumerate\")`; the range arm needs the same check.","files":null}]}